        {
         "t": "heading",
         "level": "p",
         "text": "The problem with AI logos",
         "classes": []
        },
        {
         "t": "heading",
         "level": "h2",
         "text": "Why Does My AI Logo Look Blurry When I Print It?",
         "align": "left",
         "classes": []
        },
        {
         "t": "text",
         "html": "<p>Because an AI generator gives you a picture, not a logo file. What you download is a PNG or JPG made of a fixed grid of pixels, often around 1,000 pixels across, with soft edges and compression noise built in. Stretch it onto a banner or a storefront and every one of those pixels gets bigger.</p><p>A production logo is a vector: shapes defined by curves and points, so it stays sharp at a quarter inch or twenty feet. We hear the same story from Austin owners every week. The concept is good, the file is not. That is a fixable problem, and you usually get to keep the idea you liked.</p>",
         "align": "left",
         "classes": []
        },
        {
         "t": "list",
         "items": [
          {
           "text": "Edges turn fuzzy or stair-stepped when enlarged",
           "icon": "fas fa-check-circle"
          },
          {
           "text": "Letters are misspelled, melted together or made up",
           "icon": "fas fa-check-circle"
          },
          {
           "text": "One side of the icon does not match the other",
           "icon": "fas fa-check-circle"
          },
          {
           "text": "Colors look muddy and shift between screen and print",
           "icon": "fas fa-check-circle"
          },
          {
           "text": "The “transparent” background is really a gray checkerboard",
           "icon": "fas fa-check-circle"
          }
         ],
         "classes": []
        },
        {
